<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- LUNAP'S COLOURS, HANDED TO FLUENTTHEME'S OWN RESOURCE KEYS - see
         docs/LunaP.md §48.

         WHY THIS FILE IS NOT A PILE OF STYLES. LunaP keeps <FluentTheme />, and
         always will: third-party Avalonia controls assume it is there, and
         dropping it to own the whole look would make this toolkit unusable
         beside the ecosystem it is trying to sit in. The consequence is that
         every stock control an application reaches for - a TextBox, a CheckBox,
         a Slider - works and renders in FLUENT's palette, so an application
         built mostly of form controls comes out mostly Fluent. §21.2 caught the
         same seam from the other side: three consumer sites reaching for
         SystemChromeLowColor, "a key LunaP's theme cannot reach".

         The obvious fix is a style per control, and it is the wrong one. Fluent
         paints most of these colours on template PARTS - the border inside a
         TextBox, the ellipse inside a RadioButton - which a style on the control
         cannot reach without replacing the template, and replacing the template
         means owning Fluent's keyboard handling, its focus adorners and its
         accessibility behaviour forever.

         So this does the opposite: it leaves every template exactly as Fluent
         wrote it and changes the VALUES those templates look up. Fluent's
         templates resolve these keys with DynamicResource, so overriding them
         here repaints every control that uses them, including ones this toolkit
         has never heard of and ones added to Avalonia next year.

         THE KEYS WERE READ OUT OF THE LIVE APPLICATION, not copied from a blog
         post: a probe resolved each candidate against Avalonia 12.1.0 and
         reported which existed and whether it was a Color or a Brush. Every key
         below resolved. A key that stops existing in a later Avalonia is a
         silent no-op, which is why FormControlTests asserts the OUTCOME - the
         colours a control actually paints with - rather than asserting that
         these overrides exist.

         The brushes sit outside any theme dictionary on purpose, exactly as
         Palette.axaml's do: each points at a Luna colour key that is already
         variant-keyed, so one declaration serves both columns and there is no
         second place for the two to drift apart (§2.1). -->

    <!-- Text entry. TextControlBackgroundFocused is deliberately the same
         surface as the unfocused one: Fluent flips it to solid black on focus,
         which reads as a different control rather than as the same one with the
         caret in it. The focus signal is the border, which is what the accent is
         for. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundFocused" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrushFocused" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlPlaceholderForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlSelectionHighlightColor" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />

    <!-- A dropdown's list is a surface floating over the window, so it takes the
         window surface rather than the input surface - the same distinction
         Card and SidePanel already make. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- Checked states take the accent and the glyph on top of it takes
         LunaOnAccent, which is the pairing that token exists for. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundFillChecked" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundStrokeUnchecked" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckGlyphForegroundChecked" Color="{DynamicResource LunaOnAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxForegroundUnchecked" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxForegroundChecked" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />

    <!-- THE DISABLED STATES, WHICH §48 SWEPT PAST AND §57 HAD TO COME BACK FOR.
         WCAG 1.4.11 exempts a disabled control from any contrast requirement,
         and that exemption assumes something a read-only table cell breaks: it
         assumes you do not need to READ a control you cannot use. A read-only
         check column exists to be read. Its value is information, not chrome.

         Fluent's own disabled colours are a translucent white over whatever is
         behind, and measured against LunaP's surfaces they are:

             dark   checked   white tick on #787878    4.42:1
             dark   unchecked stroke #787878           3.78:1
             light  checked   white tick on #c2c2c2    1.78:1
             light  unchecked stroke #929292           2.80:1

         The light column is the defect - a white tick on light grey is a value
         nobody can read. §57.3 carries the measurement.

         The rule these three encode: THE OUTLINE IS STRUCTURE AND DOES NOT CHANGE
         WITH ENABLEMENT; THE FILL CARRIES STATE, AND A DISABLED FILL IS MUTED
         RATHER THAN ACCENTED. So an empty box keeps LunaBorder either way
         (3.27:1 dark, 3.03:1 light - the same figures the enabled one already
         has), and a ticked-but-disabled box goes from accent to LunaMuted, which
         is the token that already means "present, not active": 4.22:1 dark and
         5.75:1 light against the surface, carrying a LunaOnAccent tick at 3.95:1
         and 6.39:1. Every figure clears 1.4.11's 3:1.

         Indeterminate takes the same pair. A LunaTable check column cannot be
         indeterminate - Checked is a Func<T, bool> - but a consumer's own
         CheckBox can, and a third state that stayed Fluent's would be the one
         illegible box left in the set. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundFillCheckedDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckGlyphForegroundCheckedDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource LunaOnAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundStrokeUncheckedDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundFillIndeterminateDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckGlyphForegroundIndeterminateDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource LunaOnAccentColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RadioButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RadioButtonOuterEllipseCheckedFill" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />

    <!-- A slider's two tracks: filled is where the value is, unfilled is where it
         is not.

         AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS COMMENT CLAIMED THE TWO ARE TOLD APART AT
         3:1, AND THEY ARE NOT - accent against border measures 1.13:1 dark and
         1.88:1 light. The claim was never measured. It is corrected here rather
         than deleted because the number it invented is the kind a reader would
         have believed and built on. §48.3.

         The standard it invoked was also the wrong one, and no theme meets it:
         Fluent's own pair is #0078D7 against #66FFFFFF, which composites to
         #787878 on this surface and measures 1.02:1 - worse than ours. A blue
         accent and a neutral track sit at almost the same relative luminance by
         construction, so clearing 3:1 between them would need a track that is
         near-white (L >= 0.65) or near-black (L <= 0.03), and a near-black track
         disappears into the surface instead.

         WHAT 1.4.11 ACTUALLY ASKS is that the control be distinguishable from
         its ADJACENT background, and both tracks clear that: accent 3.70:1 dark
         and 5.68:1 light on the surface, border 3.27:1 and 3.03:1, each pinned
         by PaletteVariantTests. Between the two tracks the difference carried is
         hue, and the value itself is carried by the thumb's position - which is
         the same thing every other theme relies on. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTrackFill" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTrackValueFill" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderThumbBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchFillOn" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchStrokeOff" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchKnobFillOn" Color="{DynamicResource LunaOnAccentColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollBarPanningThumbBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollBarTrackFill" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundChromeMediumBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- THE GAP BETWEEN A SCROLLVIEWER'S TWO SCROLLBARS, and it is here because
         of how it was found rather than because it is interesting. Three of the
         nine swept controls painted one Panel in #1F1F1F; two rounds of naming
         likely keys missed it, because a list of remembered names can only find
         what was already thought of.
 
         Enumerating the resource tree instead - 2,052 entries reachable from
         Application.Styles - gave nine keys carrying that colour, and printing the
         stray's visual ancestry named the part: Panel(PART_ScrollBarsSeparator),
         inside the ScrollViewer inside a TextBox. NumericUpDown and
         CalendarDatePicker paint it for the same reason, because both contain a
         TextBox. One key, three controls.
 
         Mapped to the surface rather than to the border, which is what Fluent
         does with it: #1F1F1F on #1E1E1E dark and #E6E6E6 on #F3F3F3 light is a
         filler that all but disappears, not a rule that divides anything. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollViewerScrollBarsSeparatorBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- The ComboBox's Border(HighlightBackground), found the same way. The first
         of these is #0078D7 in Fluent - the accent, reached through a key whose
         name says "Unfocused" - and it is why a ComboBox was the last control
         still showing Fluent's accent after every key with ComboBox in its name
         had been mapped: neither key has "Highlight" in it and the part does.
         The second is #66FFFFFF, an overlay rather than the accent, and it takes
         the border because that is the job it does. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundUnfocused" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundBorderBrushUnfocused" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- The dropdown list. Selected takes the accent and its text takes what
         goes on the accent; the pointer-over is the input surface, which is the
         same "this row, not that one" signal LunaList already uses. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemBackgroundSelected" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemBackgroundPointerOver" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemForegroundSelected" Color="{DynamicResource LunaOnAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownGlyphForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- The little button inside a TextBox that clears it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarDatePickerBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarDatePickerBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarDatePickerForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarDatePickerTextForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarDatePickerCalendarGlyphForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />

    <!-- An accent button is the one Fluent paints in the accent by design, which
         is the one case where taking the accent is the correct answer rather
         than an inherited one. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />

    <!-- ==================================================================
         §85's four kit controls, and the stock siblings they inherit from.

         WHY THESE KEYS AND NOT ONES THAT LOOK LIKE THEM. Every key below was
         read off the live ControlTheme's own Setters - the theme object
         Application.TryFindResource hands back for the type - rather than
         recalled or pattern-matched from a name. That distinction is not
         pedantry here: Fluent ALIASES many key names onto one brush INSTANCE,
         so nine different keys hand back the identical #99ffffff object. Which
         means the obvious probe - find the key whose value equals the stray
         colour - returns nine answers, eight of them wrong, and overriding any
         of the wrong ones is a silent no-op. The setter says which one the
         control actually resolves. §85.3.
         ================================================================== -->

    <!-- ListBox's Background setter names the system key directly - there is no
         semantic "ListBoxBackground" to reach for. LunaList inherits this, being
         a ListBox with StyleKeyOverride, which is how a control this toolkit
         ships came to be painting Fluent's #ff2b2b2b. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundChromeMediumLowBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- The same surface a Button rests on, deliberately: a toggle IS a button
         that stays down, and giving it a different resting colour makes it read
         as a different kind of control. ActionToggle inherits it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- Tabs. The unselected header is muted and the selected one is not, which
         is the same "this one, not that one" contrast LunaList and ComboBoxItem
         already use. The pipe is the four-pixel bar under the selected tab, and
         it was the most visible Fluent blue left in the kit - a Tabs control
         showed it on every screen it appeared on. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabItemHeaderForegroundUnselected" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabItemHeaderSelectedPipeFill" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />

    <!-- The "Ctrl+Q" hint on the right of a menu item, which is a different key
         from the item's own Foreground and is why a menu looked correct until
         an accelerator was set on it. ActionMenuItem inherits it, and a
         LunaAction with a gesture is the ordinary case rather than the rare
         one. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MenuFlyoutItemKeyboardAcceleratorTextForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />

    <!-- ==================================================================
         POPUP AND OVERLAY SURFACES - §85.8.

         Everything that opens OVER a window: the context menu Menus.Context
         builds, a flyout, a menu flyout, a tooltip, a notification card. All
         five were Fluent's #ff2b2b2b, and they are the strays that showed up
         soonest in practice, because an application whose windows are LunaP's
         greys grows a Fluent-grey menu the first time anybody right-clicks.

         They take LunaSurfaceColor rather than LunaVoidColor: a popup sits
         above the window rather than behind it, and Card and SidePanel already
         use the surface for the same "raised" reading. §85.8.
         ================================================================== -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MenuFlyoutPresenterBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MenuFlyoutPresenterBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FlyoutPresenterBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FlyoutBorderThemeBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToolTipBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToolTipForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToolTipBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NotificationCardBackgroundBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NotificationCardForegroundBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />

    <!-- ==================================================================
         STRUCTURE AND BUTTONS - §85.9.

         SOME OF THESE FLUENT KEYS HOLD A Color AND NOT A Brush, and they are
         overridden here with a brush anyway. That is deliberate and it is the
         reason this block was measured rather than reasoned about: the setters
         that read them assign to BorderBrush or Background, which take an
         IBrush, so handing back a brush satisfies them and handing back a
         Color relies on a conversion. A brush cannot be substituted where a
         template genuinely wants a Color - a gradient stop, say - so every one
         of these was checked by running the sweep afterwards rather than by
         assuming the swap was safe. §85.9 records the two that had to stay
         Colors.
         ================================================================== -->

    <!-- An expander is a card that folds, so it takes the card's surface. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ExpanderContentBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ExpanderContentBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ExpanderHeaderBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ExpanderHeaderBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ExpanderHeaderForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GroupBoxBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GroupBoxBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- SplitView's pane and DrawerPage's sheet are both "a surface that slides
         in over the window", which is the same reading as a side panel. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlPageBackgroundChromeLowBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlPageBackgroundAltHighBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NavigationBarBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- A divider is a border by another name, in both cases. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumLowBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- A link takes the accent, which is the one place in the kit where being
         the accent colour is the control's whole job rather than something it
         inherited. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HyperlinkButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaAccentColor}" />

    <!-- Both rest on the same surface a Button does, for the reason
         ToggleButtonBackground above does: they are buttons. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RepeatButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RepeatButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SplitButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SplitButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />

    <!-- THE FIVE WHOSE COLOURS ARE IN THE TEMPLATE AND NOT IN A SETTER, so the
         ControlTheme dump that named every key above says nothing about them.
         Each was found by overriding candidate keys one at a time with a
         sentinel colour and re-rendering to see which one moved - the honest
         version of "find the key that holds this colour", because the obvious
         version returns every alias. NavigationPage is the case that proves it
         matters: TWENTY-SIX keys resolve to its #99ffffff and exactly one of
         them repaints the control. §85.9. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemAltMediumLowColor" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NavigationControlNavBarBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NavigationBarForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PipsPagerNavigationButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaMutedColor}" />

    <!-- The border colour rather than the muted one, and the difference is the
         point: a disabled arrow that is the same grey as an enabled one says
         nothing. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PipsPagerNavigationButtonForegroundDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- Fluent's validation text is YELLOW (#fffff000). This palette has an
         error colour and it is not that, so a form that failed validation was
         telling the user in a colour the rest of the application never uses. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlErrorTextForegroundBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaErrorColor}" />

    <!-- ==================================================================
         DATE AND TIME - §85.10.

         The heaviest cluster, and the one an application meets as soon as it
         asks for a date. A DatePicker is a button that opens a flyout onto a
         Calendar, so it is three surfaces rather than one, and all three were
         Fluent's: the button #66000000, the flyout #ff2b2b2b, the calendar
         grid #ff1f1f1f with today marked in #ff0078d7.
         ================================================================== -->

    <!-- The closed picker is a button that says a date, so it rests on the same
         input surface a TextBox does - it is an input, not a command. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DatePickerButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DatePickerButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DatePickerButtonBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TimePickerButtonBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TimePickerButtonForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TimePickerButtonBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- The opened flyout is a popup, so it takes the popup surface the block
         above gave every other popup. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DatePickerFlyoutPresenterBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DatePickerFlyoutPresenterBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TimePickerFlyoutPresenterBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TimePickerFlyoutPresenterBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarViewBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarViewForeground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaTextColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarViewBorderBrush" Color="{DynamicResource LunaBorderColor}" />

    <!-- The days either side of the month on show. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CalendarViewOutOfScopeBackground" Color="{DynamicResource LunaSurfaceColor}" />

    <!-- TODAY'S CELL, and it took a visual-tree walk to find. It is not a setter
         on CalendarDayButton and not reachable by searching for the colour: the
         differential probe tried all forty keys that resolve to #ff0078d7 and
         none of them moved it, because the setter lives in a style NESTED
         inside `^:today`, targeting Border#Root, and reads SystemAccentColor -
         a Color, not a brush, and one the probe's own override could not
         reach. Walking the realised tree to ask which Border was blue, then
         reading the theme that owns it, is what answered it. §85.10. -->
    <!-- AND IT HAS TO BE A Color, WHICH IS THE ONE CASE THIS FILE'S USUAL TRICK
         CANNOT DO. Every other override here is a SolidColorBrush pointing at a
         variant-keyed Luna colour with DynamicResource, which is what keeps one
         declaration serving both columns. SystemAccentColor cannot be one:
         Fluent reads it where a Color is genuinely required, and substituting a
         brush does not degrade quietly - it throws InvalidCastException and
         takes the control's whole render down with it. Measured, not guessed:
         the brush version turned five controls red - MeterRow, ProgressBar,
         RadioButton and both picker presenters - each with
         "Unable to cast object of type 'Avalonia.Media.SolidColorBrush' to type
         'Avalonia.Media.Color'".

         So it is an ALIAS instead, one per variant, which is the same idiom
         Fluent uses internally and the reason so many of its key names hand
         back one shared brush instance. §85.10. -->
    <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
            <StaticResource x:Key="SystemAccentColor" ResourceKey="LunaAccentColor" />
        </ResourceDictionary>
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
            <StaticResource x:Key="SystemAccentColor" ResourceKey="LunaAccentColor" />
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

</ResourceDictionary>
